How is "Therefore, Mr Barroso, my group will not support your guidelines or your staffing proposal." said in Finnish?
    
[The Start of Assistant 1’s Answer]
In Finnish, the sentence "Therefore, Mr Barroso, my group will not support your guidelines or your staffing proposal." can be translated as:

"Siksi, herra Barroso, ryhmäni ei tue ohjeitasi tai henkilöstöehdotustasi."

Here's a breakdown of the translation:

- Siksi - Therefore
- Herra - Mister (in this case, Mr.)
- Barroso - Barroso (name of the person being addressed; the name stays the same in Finnish)
- Ryhmäni - My group (possessive form of "group" with "my" included in the word)
- Ei - Not (used for negation)
- Tue - Support (verb in third-person singular form, in this case meaning "will not support")
- Ohjeitasi - Your guidelines (plural form of "guideline" with the possessive "your" included, meaning "your guidelines")
- Tai - Or
- Henkilöstöehdotustasi - Your staffing proposal (compound word: "henkilöstö" means "staff" or "personnel", "ehdotus" means "proposal", and adding "-tasi" at the end indicates the possessive "your", making it "your staffing proposal")

So, putting all of these together, you get: "Siksi, herra Barroso, ryhmäni ei tue ohjeitasi tai henkilöstöehdotustasi."
[The End of Assistant 1’s Answer]
